## 1) Severity: HIGH | Type: Bug
Title: Retry-count cleanup deletes reminders of every method, not just SMS

Affected:
- packages/features/ee/workflows/api/scheduleSMSReminders.ts:31-43

Explanation:
The new `OR` branch `{ retryCount: { gt: 1 } }` has no `method` filter. This SMS cron job will therefore delete any `WorkflowReminder` whose `retryCount > 1`, including EMAIL and WHATSAPP reminders.

Right now only this handler increments `retryCount`. Once the email or WhatsApp schedulers adopt the same field, which the generic column name invites, this job will silently delete their reminders. Those reminders may still be pending or managed by another cron.

Scope the condition to SMS.

Proposed fix:
~~~ts
OR: [
  {
    method: WorkflowMethods.SMS,
    scheduledDate: { lte: dayjs().toISOString() },
  },
  {
    method: WorkflowMethods.SMS,
    retryCount: { gt: 1 },
  },
],
~~~

## 2) Severity: MEDIUM | Type: Reliability
Title: Unguarded DB update in catch block can abort the whole batch

Affected:
- packages/features/ee/workflows/api/scheduleSMSReminders.ts:189-199

Explanation:
The `prisma.workflowReminder.update` inside `catch` is not protected. It can throw, for example:
- `P2025` if the reminder was deleted concurrently (by booking cancellation, or by the `deleteMany` in a parallel cron run),
- on a DB connectivity error, which is often the very reason the `try` failed in the first place.

If it throws, the error escapes the loop. All remaining reminders go unprocessed, the handler returns a 500, and the original error is never logged because `console.log` runs after the update.

Log first, and wrap the update in its own try/catch. `updateMany` is another option, since it does not throw when no row matches.

Proposed fix:
~~~ts
} catch (error) {
  console.log(`Error scheduling SMS with error ${error}`);
  try {
    await prisma.workflowReminder.updateMany({
      where: { id: reminder.id },
      data: { retryCount: { increment: 1 } },
    });
  } catch (e) {
    console.log(`Failed to update retryCount for reminder ${reminder.id}: ${e}`);
  }
}
~~~

## 3) Severity: MEDIUM | Type: Bug
Title: Reminders already scheduled with Twilio can be counted as failures and deleted

Affected:
- packages/features/ee/workflows/api/scheduleSMSReminders.ts:189-197
- packages/features/ee/workflows/api/scheduleSMSReminders.ts:38-42

Explanation:
The `catch` covers the whole `try` block. If `twilio.scheduleSMS` succeeds but the later `update` that sets `scheduled: true` and `referenceId` throws, the reminder is counted as failed and `retryCount` is incremented. The next run will schedule the SMS again, producing a duplicate message. After two such failures, the row is deleted while the message is still queued at Twilio. With `referenceId` lost, the message can no longer be cancelled when the booking is cancelled or rescheduled.

Only count a retry when the Twilio call itself fails. Alternatively, persist the `referenceId` before treating anything as a failure.

Proposed fix:
~~~ts
let scheduledSMS;
try {
  scheduledSMS = await twilio.scheduleSMS(/* ... */);
} catch (e) {
  await incrementRetry(reminder.id);
  continue;
}
if (scheduledSMS) {
  await prisma.workflowReminder.update({ /* scheduled: true, referenceId */ });
} else {
  await incrementRetry(reminder.id);
}
~~~

## 4) Severity: LOW | Type: Maintainability
Title: Use atomic increment and extract the duplicated retry threshold logic

Affected:
- packages/features/ee/workflows/api/scheduleSMSReminders.ts:178-186
- packages/features/ee/workflows/api/scheduleSMSReminders.ts:190-197
- packages/features/ee/workflows/api/scheduleSMSReminders.ts:39-41

Explanation:
`retryCount: reminder.retryCount + 1` is a read-modify-write on a stale value. If two cron invocations overlap, increments are lost. Prisma's `{ increment: 1 }` is atomic.

The update is also duplicated in two places.

The cutoff `gt: 1` is a magic number. In practice it allows only two failed attempts before deletion. Deletion is silent, so users never learn their reminder was dropped.

Extract a helper and a named `MAX_SMS_RETRIES` constant, and log when reminders are dropped.

Proposed fix:
~~~ts
const MAX_SMS_RETRIES = 2;
const incrementRetry = (id: number) =>
  prisma.workflowReminder.update({ where: { id }, data: { retryCount: { increment: 1 } } });
// deleteMany: retryCount: { gte: MAX_SMS_RETRIES }
~~~

## 5) Severity: LOW | Type: Performance
Title: Unindexed `retryCount` predicate in an OR forces a table scan on every cron run

Affected:
- packages/features/ee/workflows/api/scheduleSMSReminders.ts:31-43
- packages/prisma/schema.prisma:1000

Explanation:
Postgres cannot satisfy `(method = 'SMS' AND scheduledDate <= now) OR retryCount > 1` from the existing indexes. As a result, every cron invocation is likely to scan the whole `WorkflowReminder` table, which can be large.

Scope the condition to SMS (see issue 1) and add a supporting index. Another option is to split the cleanup into two separate `deleteMany` calls.

Proposed fix:
~~~prisma
@@index([method, retryCount])
~~~
